      "role": "user",
      "content": "I understand the regulatory caution, but \"tiered underwriting\" sounds like we're just finding a way to dress up the same fundamental risk assessment. If the data is telling us that a specific demographic trajectory is the single most consistent predictor of a crime spike, isn't it a bit of a mathematical stretch to weigh it equally with \"quality of local services\" or \"economic factors\" if those variables aren't showing the same immediate, high-magnitude correlation? \n\nIf we're being truly rigorous and not just trying to be \"responsible\" for the sake of optics, shouldn't we prioritize the highest-yield signal? If the \"demographic transition\" is the actual driver of the risk, then treating it as just one of many equally important variables actually decreases the precision of our model. From a pure actuarial standpoint, why shouldn't the most direct and historically accurate signal be allowed to dictate the primary tier of our underwriting?",
